    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. we've been having some issues with the top layers shifting slightly even when they seem balanced. is there a specific interlocking pattern you'd recommend to prevent that? like, a specific way to overlap the bricks or blocks so the load doesn't migrate during transport or when the racks are being moved?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In high-density racking systems, employing well-established interlocking patterns can prevent load migration and shifting. One technique involves using pallet blocks with staggered notches that allow each block to fit snugly into the next, distributing the weight more evenly across subsequent layers. Consider the use of solobar blocks or wooden runners; they interlock tightly and have a reduced void space which diminishes the possibility of shifting. A staggered patterned approach, like the \"brick wall,\" where the pallets are laid in an offset zigzag fashion, can further enhance stability. It's also important to ensure that the orientation of the stacking pattern is consistent across the entire warehouse to prevent any lateral movement when pallets are moved or shifted. Regularly inspecting and maintaining the stability of the stacked items is crucial, and many warehouses consider the implementation of automated systems for repositioning pallets to mitigate this issue on heavier shifts permanently.",
